WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.301 --> 00:00:03.035
-Our next guest is
an award-winning author

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:05.004
whose new novel, "Eleutheria,"
is available now.

00:00:05.004 --> 00:00:08.274
Please welcome to the show
Allegra Hyde, everybody.

00:00:08.274 --> 00:00:10.176
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:19.952 --> 00:00:21.354
Welcome to the show.

00:00:21.354 --> 00:00:22.955
First of all, I want to start
by saying you're also from

00:00:22.955 --> 00:00:25.625
New Hampshire, and I'm so happy
to have a Granite Stater here.

00:00:25.625 --> 00:00:27.627
-Yeah, it's amazing to meet

00:00:27.627 --> 00:00:29.062
another person
outside of the state.

00:00:29.062 --> 00:00:31.364
-Yeah. Do you feel like
New Hampshire has,

00:00:31.364 --> 00:00:33.800
in any way,
informed your work as a writer?

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:36.069
-Definitely. I mean,
I grew up out in the woods,

00:00:36.069 --> 00:00:37.570
where, you know,
bears were roaming around,

00:00:37.570 --> 00:00:39.005
getting into our garage.

00:00:39.005 --> 00:00:43.910
And so I came in close contact
with nature, grew to respect it,

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:46.546
to fear it, and I think
that informs my writing

00:00:46.546 --> 00:00:47.780
in this book.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:50.250
-So, this book is about
sort of a near future

00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:52.552
where climate change
is a little bit worse,

00:00:52.552 --> 00:00:55.554
which might be
the case in the near future.

00:00:55.554 --> 00:00:59.191
What made you choose that
as a jumping-off point?

00:00:59.191 --> 00:01:01.694
-Well, I knew I wanted to write
about climate change

00:01:01.694 --> 00:01:04.530
and engage with this issue,

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:09.402
and it felt like the near future
was a way to think ahead,

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:11.738
to kind of see
where we're heading,

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:14.840
but not be so far into
a doomsday scenario,

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:17.410
where we can't really
connect with it emotionally.

00:01:17.410 --> 00:01:20.480
And so by having
it in that near future,

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.050
I could think through
what's gonna happen

00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:27.686
and then, more importantly,
how we might problem-solve

00:01:27.686 --> 00:01:29.122
and how we might mobilize to --

00:01:29.122 --> 00:01:31.191
-Because there is some --
I think people

00:01:31.191 --> 00:01:33.326
sometimes worry
when they hear that a book

00:01:33.326 --> 00:01:35.628
is about the
future and climate change,

00:01:35.628 --> 00:01:37.663
that there won't be
any optimism in it,

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:40.366
but there is a message
of hope in here.

00:01:40.366 --> 00:01:41.967
Is that fair to say?

00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:47.273
-Yeah. I think
I wanted to show readers

00:01:47.273 --> 00:01:49.675
what it might look like
and what it might take

00:01:49.675 --> 00:01:52.412
to come together
to solve this crisis.

00:01:52.412 --> 00:01:56.281
It's really a book about
activism, ultimately.

00:01:56.281 --> 00:02:01.187
I'm of the belief that
we do have the tools right now

00:02:01.187 --> 00:02:02.988
to solve climate change.

00:02:02.988 --> 00:02:05.090
It's just a
matter of using them.

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:09.562
-"Eleutheria" refers to a
utopian society in this book.

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:11.764
What is the sort of
history of that name?

00:02:11.764 --> 00:02:13.866
Where did you --
How did you land on that?

00:02:13.866 --> 00:02:19.138
-So, there is a real island
in the Bahamas called Eleuthera.

00:02:19.138 --> 00:02:22.908
I named my island Eleutheria
because I wanted to show how

00:02:22.908 --> 00:02:26.912
the world in my book is slightly
exaggerated, slightly distorted,

00:02:26.912 --> 00:02:29.715
not quite our world

00:02:29.715 --> 00:02:32.952
and because it refers to
the original name

00:02:32.952 --> 00:02:36.856
that the island had when some
Puritan settlers

00:02:36.856 --> 00:02:42.262
showed up there, wanted to start
a utopian community,

00:02:42.262 --> 00:02:43.495
and that's the name
that they used.

00:02:43.495 --> 00:02:44.797
It eventually changed slightly.

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:47.667
So it's kind of heralding back
to a group of people

00:02:47.667 --> 00:02:50.336
who arrived in this place
with an ideal,

00:02:50.336 --> 00:02:53.172
and then things went awry,
as they often do.

00:02:53.172 --> 00:02:56.008
-So, as a writer,
have been drawn to writing about

00:02:56.008 --> 00:02:58.744
utopian societies and sort of
the idea that they --

00:02:58.744 --> 00:03:00.346
the center often doesn't hold.

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:03.482
They fall apart pretty quickly
when people go into them

00:03:03.482 --> 00:03:06.619
with utopian expectations,
I guess.

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:11.590
-Yeah, I'm really fascinated by
that conflict between

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:13.859
idealism and practicality.

00:03:13.859 --> 00:03:17.263
I also have always been
drawn to utopian communities

00:03:17.263 --> 00:03:21.267
because I respect the fact that
these groups of people,

00:03:21.267 --> 00:03:24.671
these individuals are willing to
step away from the mainstream

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:26.872
and try something different,

00:03:26.872 --> 00:03:30.843
knowing that it probably will
fail, but they're trying.

00:03:30.843 --> 00:03:33.245
-You've spent some time
in some of these communities

00:03:33.245 --> 00:03:35.014
that maybe informed
your writing.

00:03:35.014 --> 00:03:36.048
Was it a hippie commune?

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:37.383
Is that a fair way
to describe it?

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:41.153
-Yeah, I spent time
in a lot of hippie communes

00:03:41.153 --> 00:03:42.654
in New Zealand, for instance.

00:03:42.654 --> 00:03:44.156
There's more hippie communes
there, per capita,

00:03:44.156 --> 00:03:45.258
than anywhere else.

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:46.392
-That makes sense.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:47.660
That seems like
a place where, like --

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:48.828
I feel like if I landed there,
I'd be like,

00:03:48.828 --> 00:03:50.162
"I think I'm gonna be a hippie."

00:03:50.162 --> 00:03:53.665
And what about -- Not a current,
not an active sex cult,

00:03:53.665 --> 00:03:55.167
but a former sex cult?

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:56.536
What's the distinction there?

00:03:56.536 --> 00:04:00.606
-Well, this former sex cult
had rebranded

00:04:00.606 --> 00:04:03.676
as a health and wellness center.

00:04:03.676 --> 00:04:07.447
-How many people
stayed on after the rebrand?

00:04:07.447 --> 00:04:09.314
-There were definitely some
people lingering,

00:04:09.314 --> 00:04:11.184
and that made it
a little creepy.

00:04:11.184 --> 00:04:12.685
-Yeah. And they kind of
were saying stuff like,

00:04:12.685 --> 00:04:15.020
"Used to be a lot
more fun around here."

00:04:15.020 --> 00:04:17.457
-Yeah, that's pretty much
what it was.

00:04:18.658 --> 00:04:20.159
-"This health-and-wellness
thing's alright,

00:04:20.159 --> 00:04:22.028
but it used to be
a real banger around here."

00:04:22.028 --> 00:04:23.662
-Yeah.

00:04:23.662 --> 00:04:25.831
-Now, you are also a teacher.

00:04:25.831 --> 00:04:28.434
You're a college professor
at Oberlin in Ohio,

00:04:28.434 --> 00:04:29.668
a creative-writing professor.

00:04:29.668 --> 00:04:30.803
-That's right.

00:04:30.803 --> 00:04:32.538
-How do you like
dealing with students?

00:04:32.538 --> 00:04:34.607
Do you do you enjoy that,
talking to young writers?

00:04:34.607 --> 00:04:37.209
-Yeah, it's amazing.
They're so inspiring.

00:04:37.209 --> 00:04:38.945
Oberlin students
are really incredible.

00:04:38.945 --> 00:04:41.179
So I'm so grateful to
have that job.

00:04:41.179 --> 00:04:44.984
-And is it true that
you should be teaching tonight?

00:04:44.984 --> 00:04:47.854
-Yeah. You caught me.
I'm not in class.

00:04:47.854 --> 00:04:48.954
-Will your students
respect the fact that

00:04:48.954 --> 00:04:51.123
you are missing class
to be here?

00:04:51.123 --> 00:04:55.361
-I hope so.
-You didn't prepare them for it?

00:04:55.361 --> 00:04:57.396
-They -- Some of them know.
-Yeah, I gotcha.

00:04:57.396 --> 00:04:59.065
And the rest are gonna hopefully
find out in real time.

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:00.299
-Yeah.

00:05:00.299 --> 00:05:03.303
-They're gonna be like,
"She doesn't have COVID."

00:05:04.737 --> 00:05:07.173
Hey, I'm glad about that
and I'm glad you're here.

00:05:07.173 --> 00:05:08.241
Congratulations on the book.

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:09.442
So lovely talking to you,
Allegra.

00:05:09.442 --> 00:05:12.711
-Thank you.
-You guys, that's Allegra Hyde.

00:05:12.711 --> 00:05:15.448
"Eleutheria" is available
wherever you buy your books,

00:05:15.448 --> 00:05:16.815
but please support local
and independent bookstores.

00:05:16.815 --> 00:05:17.884
We'll be right back.

